# No Shabbat / Yom Tov phone use

Mitz Mode **turns itself off** on Shabbat and Yom Tov (melacha-forbidden days). The user **cannot and does not** use the app then.

## Hard rules for agents

- **Never** spend time on Shabbat or Yom Tov “what if they’re using the phone” edge cases.
- **Never** add weekday-siddur / checklist / UI logic whose only purpose is Shabbat or Yom Tov davening or in-app use that day.
- Treat Shabbat and Yom Tov (including both days of Rosh Hashanah, Yom Kippur, etc.) as **out of scope for runtime app use**.
- When gating liturgy or features by calendar, reason about **weekday / Motzei / Erev** windows the user can actually open the app — not “during Shabbat Amidah” or “during YK machzor on the phone.”

## Examples of wasted work (do not do)

- Special-casing silent Amidah / Tachanun / inserts “if today is Shabbat”
- Motzei-unlock flows whose only goal is phone use while still tagged Shabbat/YT for davening
- Debating Israel vs chutz phone use **on** Yom Tov itself

## What is still in scope

- **Erev** Shabbat/YT and **Motzei** (after tzeit) weekday behavior — the app is on
- Calendar labels, prep checklists, and “tonight begins…” logic that run **before** or **after** the forbidden day
- Knowing a day **is** Shabbat/YT so the app stays off / hides phone features — not so the siddur works that day
